{
 slug:'teen-life-and-free-time',title:'Teen Life and Free Time',level:'B1',age:'13-15',minutes:60,
 skill:'Speaking',secondary:'Vocabulary',tech:'Board only',style:'Communicative',summary:'Compare two hobbies, explain a preference, and negotiate a club choice.',
 objective:'Compare two free-time activities and explain a preference with at least two reasons, then respond to a different view.',
 vocab:[['hobby','An activity you enjoy in your free time.','Drawing is my favorite hobby.'],['relax','Become calmer after effort or stress.','I read to relax.'],['practice','Repeat something to improve.','I practice guitar twice a week.'],['prefer','Like one choice more than another.','I prefer chess to running.'],['challenge','Something difficult that tests your ability.','Learning a new song is a challenge.']],
 frames:['I prefer ___ to ___ because ___.','Both activities ___, but only ___ .','I see your point; however, ___ .'],
 reading:{title:'Two Ways to Spend Friday',text:'At a fictional school, students can try one of two Friday clubs. The art club meets in a quiet classroom. Members share pencils and paper, so they do not need to buy supplies. They can draw alone or help a partner. There is no final competition.\n\nThe running club meets in the school yard. Students practice in small groups and choose their own pace. It is not a race, but each student sets a personal distance goal. When it rains, the club uses the covered court.\n\nLina prefers art because drawing helps her relax after a busy week. She also likes making a picture she can keep. Omar prefers running. He enjoys moving after sitting in lessons, and reaching a goal feels like a challenge worth accepting.\n\nLina thinks running sounds tiring. Omar thinks a quiet room might help him concentrate. They agree to try each other’s club once before making a final choice. Neither believes that one hobby is best for everybody.',qa:[['Why do art members not need to buy supplies?','They share pencils and paper.','Members share pencils and paper, so they do not need to buy supplies.'],['What happens to running club when it rains?','It uses the covered court.','When it rains, the club uses the covered court.'],['Give one reason Lina prefers art.','Drawing helps her relax.','Lina prefers art because drawing helps her relax after a busy week.'],['How do they keep an open mind?','They agree to try each other’s club.','They agree to try each other’s club once before making a final choice.']]},
 listening:{title:'Choosing a New Hobby',script:'My name is Maya, and I have been thinking about my free time. Last term, I spent most afternoons doing the same things. This term, I want to try something different. My two choices are a chess group and a dance group at school. Both groups meet on Wednesday, so I cannot join both.\n\nI enjoy a challenge, and chess would make me think carefully. My friend says I would need to practice regularly. I like that idea, but I sometimes find it hard to sit still after a full day of lessons. Dance would give me a chance to move. I could also meet people outside my usual class.\n\nAt first, I preferred chess because I already knew one person in the group. Then I tried one dance session. I made a few mistakes, but the other students were kind. I felt more relaxed by the end.\n\nI have decided to join dance for this term. My main reasons are movement and meeting new people. I still want to play chess sometimes, perhaps with my friend during a break. Choosing one hobby now does not mean I must give up every other interest forever.',qa:[['Why can Maya not join both groups?','They meet on the same day','Both groups meet on Wednesday, so I cannot join both.',['They meet on the same day','Both cost too much','Her friends refuse']],['Why did she prefer chess at first?','She knew someone in the group','I already knew one person in the group',['She knew someone in the group','She disliked movement','She had won a tournament']],['What are her main reasons for choosing dance?','Movement and meeting new people','My main reasons are movement and meeting new people.',['Movement and meeting new people','Winning prizes and traveling','Being alone and saving money']]]},
 practiceA:[['Complete: I prefer reading ___ watching a film.','to'],['Join with because: I choose art. It helps me relax.','I choose art because it helps me relax.'],['Compare using quieter: Art club / running club.','Art club is quieter than running club.'],['Use both to describe art and running as hobbies.','Both art and running are hobbies.'],['Respond politely: Running is the best hobby for everyone.','I see your point, but different people enjoy different hobbies.']],
 practiceB:[['Correct: I prefer dance than chess.','I prefer dance to chess.'],['Join with although: Chess is difficult. I enjoy it.','Although chess is difficult, I enjoy it.'],['Compare using more active: Dance / chess.','Dance is more active than chess.'],['Ask a partner for a reason for their preference.','Why do you prefer that hobby?'],['Disagree politely with: Quiet hobbies are boring.','I understand, but quiet hobbies help some people relax.']],
 applyA:[['Compare art and running using one similarity.','Both take practice and can be enjoyed with other people.'],['Give one difference supported by the reading.','Art uses a quiet classroom; running uses the yard or covered court.'],['Choose one club and give two reasons.','Example: I prefer art because it is calm and supplies are shared.'],['Ask a follow-up question about a partner’s hobby.','How often do you practice?'],['Suggest a compromise for friends with different hobbies.','They could try each other’s activities on different days.']],
 applyB:[['Compare chess and dance using one similarity.','Both require practice.'],['Explain one advantage of joining a new group.','You can meet people outside your usual class.'],['Give a reason someone might prefer a quiet hobby.','It may help them concentrate or relax.'],['Respond to a partner who prefers a different activity.','I understand your reason. I prefer dance because I enjoy movement.'],['Give a 45-second recommendation with a limitation.','Recommend one hobby, give two relevant reasons, and recognize that preferences differ.']],
 activity:'Design our Friday club',cards:['Choice 1: Art. Shared supplies; quiet room; individual or paired work.','Choice 2: Running. Small groups; personal pace; covered space if it rains.','Decision: Choose a club trial for your group. Give two reasons and one reason another group might choose differently.'],model:'We prefer art because it is quiet and everyone can use shared supplies. Running is more active, so another group might prefer it. We could try running next month.',
 challenge:['Compare a third hobby without claiming it suits everybody.','Summarize a partner’s reasons before giving your own view.'],caution:'Offer fictional preferences so nobody must disclose personal schedules, money or athletic ability. Avoid ranking hobbies by gender.',homework:['Write 90–110 words comparing two hobbies.','Use prefer, because and one comparison; include a different possible opinion.']
},
{
 slug:'solving-everyday-problems',title:'Solving Everyday Problems',level:'B2',age:'13-15',minutes:60,
 skill:'Mixed',secondary:'Critical Thinking',tech:'Projector + audio',style:'Task-based',summary:'Weigh competing solutions against time, fairness, cost, and likely consequences.',
 objective:'Describe a practical problem, evaluate two solutions against explicit criteria, and justify a group decision while acknowledging a drawback.',
 vocab:[['solution','A way to address a problem.','Our solution is to divide the work.'],['decision','A choice made after consideration.','The decision needs a clear reason.'],['consequence','A result of an action or choice.','A possible consequence is extra work.'],['agree','Share or accept an opinion.','I agree that fairness matters.'],['disagree','Have a different opinion.','I disagree because the plan excludes one person.']],
 frames:['If we ___, the likely consequence is ___ .','I agree about ___, but we also need to consider ___ .','On balance, ___ is more feasible because ___ .'],
 reading:{title:'The Display Deadline',text:'This is a fictional school problem. Four students must finish a history display by Friday. They have one lunch meeting left and cannot stay after school. Their first plan used a large printed map, but the print shop has told them that it will not be ready until Monday. They have paper, markers and their research notes. Their teacher says accuracy and clear explanations matter more than expensive materials.\n\nJon suggests drawing the map by hand. This would keep the original design, but the group is worried that drawing details will take most of the meeting. A simpler map would still show the three places mentioned in their research.\n\nAsha proposes replacing the map with three labeled information panels. Each panel would explain one place and include a small location sketch. Everyone could complete a panel or check the facts. However, visitors might find it harder to see the relationship between the places.\n\nThe group agrees to compare time, clarity and fair participation. They have not yet made a final decision. Before choosing, they need to estimate the work and identify a way to reduce each plan’s main weakness. They cannot solve the deadline problem simply by spending more money.',qa:[['Why is the original plan no longer feasible?','The map will arrive after the deadline.','it will not be ready until Monday'],['What matters most to the teacher?','Accuracy and clear explanations.','accuracy and clear explanations matter more than expensive materials'],['What is a drawback of the panels?','Visitors may not see the relationship between places easily.','visitors might find it harder to see the relationship between the places'],['Has the group chosen its final solution?','No.','They have not yet made a final decision.']]},
 listening:{title:'A Different Way to Rehearse',script:'Our group has a problem with tomorrow’s class presentation. We planned to meet in the music room after lunch, but another class is using it. We still need to practice together, and we cannot stay after school. There are four people in our group, and one of them needs a quiet place to hear the lines clearly.\n\nOne suggestion is to practice in the yard. There is plenty of space, but it can be noisy. Another suggestion is to use an empty classroom for fifteen minutes during lunch. That would be quieter, although our rehearsal time would be shorter. We would need to ask the teacher before using the room.\n\nI initially preferred the yard because we could start immediately. After listening to the others, I changed my mind. A rehearsal is not useful if one person cannot follow it. We agreed to ask for the classroom and practice only the opening and the two most difficult transitions.\n\nIf the room is not available, our backup is to read the lines quietly in pairs and meet for five minutes to check the transitions. That is not ideal, but it gives everyone a role. Our decision puts clear communication ahead of having the longest possible rehearsal.',qa:[['What makes the yard less suitable?','Noise may prevent one person from following','it can be noisy',['Noise may prevent one person from following','It has no space','It is closed tomorrow']],['What will the group practice in the classroom?','The opening and two difficult transitions','practice only the opening and the two most difficult transitions',['The opening and two difficult transitions','Every line three times','Only the ending']],['What is their priority?','Clear communication','Our decision puts clear communication ahead of having the longest possible rehearsal.',['Clear communication','The longest rehearsal','Avoiding permission']]]},
 practiceA:[['State the display problem without blaming a teammate.','The printed map will arrive after Friday’s deadline.'],['Write a conditional consequence for drawing a detailed map.','If we draw every detail, we may run out of time.'],['Agree with a concern about time, then introduce fairness.','I agree that time is limited, but everyone also needs a useful role.'],['Disagree respectfully with buying more materials.','I disagree because spending more will not change the deadline.'],['Suggest a way to reduce the panels’ main weakness.','Add a small shared overview sketch showing all three places.']],
 practiceB:[['Describe the rehearsal problem in one neutral sentence.','The planned room is unavailable and the group still needs a rehearsal.'],['Write a conditional consequence for using a noisy yard.','If we use the yard, one person may not hear the lines.'],['Qualify a claim that the classroom is perfect.','The classroom is quieter, but the available time is shorter.'],['Suggest a fair backup if the room is unavailable.','Practice in pairs and check transitions together briefly.'],['Explain why permission is part of feasibility.','The plan cannot work unless the teacher allows the group to use the room.']],
 applyA:[['Rank time, clarity and participation for the display. Explain your order.','Accept a justified ranking that addresses all three criteria.'],['Give one benefit and one risk of a simple hand-drawn map.','It shows relationships, but drawing may still consume limited time.'],['Give one benefit and one risk of three panels.','Tasks can be shared, but connections between places may be unclear.'],['Choose a plan and name a safeguard.','Example: Use panels with a shared overview sketch and a fact checker.'],['State what information could change your choice.','A realistic time estimate or a sample showing that the simple map is quick.']],
 applyB:[['Compare yard and classroom against inclusion.','The classroom better supports someone who needs quiet.'],['Identify a constraint the group cannot change.','They cannot stay after school.'],['Assign four roles for a short rehearsal.','Speaker, partner speaker, timekeeper and transition observer; rotate if appropriate.'],['Justify a backup with a drawback.','Pair practice includes everyone, although it does not simulate the full presentation.'],['Give a 60-second recommendation and answer one objection.','Use a clear recommendation, two criteria, one drawback and a feasible response.']],
 activity:'The decision meeting',cards:['Problem: Four students, one lunch meeting, Friday deadline, delayed printed map. Supplies: paper, markers, research notes.','Option A: Draw a simple map with three locations. Option B: Create three information panels and a shared sketch.','Roles: Facilitator, timekeeper, evidence checker and inclusion checker. Compare time, clarity and participation.'],model:'On balance, we recommend three panels with one small overview sketch. This divides the work fairly and keeps the facts clear. The sketch reduces the risk that visitors miss the connections.',
 challenge:['Defend the solution you did not initially prefer.','Add one new constraint and explain exactly how it changes your decision.'],caution:'Assess the reasoning, not agreement with a preferred answer. Critical Thinking is demonstrated by criteria, consequences and a justified revision.',homework:['Write a 140–170-word recommendation about the display.','Compare both options, acknowledge a drawback and explain a safeguard.']
},
{
 slug:'globalization-and-culture',title:'Globalization and Culture',level:'C1',age:'16-18',minutes:75,
 skill:'Writing',secondary:'Speaking',tech:'Projector / screen',style:'Discussion-led',summary:'Distinguish access from control and write a qualified argument about cultural exchange.',
 objective:'Write a balanced argument about cultural exchange, distinguishing wider access from local control and addressing a counterargument.',
 vocab:[['globalization','Increasing connections between places and societies.','Globalization can widen an artist’s audience.'],['identity','A sense of who a person or community is.','Identity can change without disappearing.'],['heritage','Traditions and practices passed between generations.','The festival celebrates local heritage.'],['diversity','The presence of different experiences and forms of expression.','A diverse program includes more than one viewpoint.'],['integration','Participation in a shared setting while negotiating differences.','Integration need not require identical tastes.']],
 frames:['Although wider access may ___, it does not necessarily ___ .','The relevant distinction is between ___ and ___ .','A defensible compromise would ___, provided that ___ .'],
 reading:{title:'Who Shapes the Festival?',text:'The town and festival in this case are fictional. Luma’s annual music festival once served mainly local residents. A partnership with an international distributor would now let performers reach listeners abroad. The distributor offers translation, promotion and payment for selected recordings. For some musicians, this is an opportunity to earn money while continuing work that local ticket sales cannot support.\n\nThe offer also has conditions. The distributor wants shorter performances, familiar genre labels and a program that can be promoted consistently across different markets. These requirements are not an explicit ban on local traditions. Nevertheless, they may favor pieces that are easier for unfamiliar listeners to recognize. Musicians whose work depends on long storytelling sequences worry that selection would reward only a narrow part of their heritage.\n\nThe youth council sees another possibility. Members already combine influences from different places, and they object to treating culture as a fixed collection of old practices. They want new collaborations to count as part of local identity. Some elders agree, while asking who will have the authority to approve how a performance is described. The disagreement is therefore not simply between young innovators and older guardians.\n\nA proposed agreement would reserve some programming decisions for a local committee, publish the selection criteria and give performers a chance to correct translations. It might protect meaningful participation, but it would also add costs and delay. The distributor has not accepted these terms.\n\nThe case separates two questions that are often merged: whether cultural work travels more widely, and whether the people who create it retain influence over its presentation. Wider circulation may support diversity, yet circulation alone does not guarantee control. A convincing judgment needs to examine both opportunities and the rules that distribute decision-making power.',qa:[['What concrete benefits does the distributor offer?','Translation, promotion and payment for selected recordings.','The distributor offers translation, promotion and payment for selected recordings.'],['Why might selection narrow the representation of heritage?','Market-friendly formats may favor easily recognized pieces.','they may favor pieces that are easier for unfamiliar listeners to recognize'],['Why is the conflict not simply young versus old?','Some elders support collaboration while questioning decision-making authority.','Some elders agree, while asking who will have the authority to approve how a performance is described.'],['What distinction structures the final paragraph?','Wider circulation versus creators’ influence over presentation.','whether cultural work travels more widely, and whether the people who create it retain influence over its presentation']]},
 listening:{title:'A Curator’s Conditional Support',script:'I support the proposed festival partnership, but my support is conditional. Reaching listeners abroad could help musicians continue their work, and translation could make unfamiliar traditions more accessible. It would be a mistake to assume that every commercial partnership necessarily destroys local culture.\n\nHowever, access is only one part of the question. If an outside organization chooses every performance and writes every description, the community may become visible without gaining much influence. A recording can travel widely while presenting a very narrow picture of the people who made it.\n\nMy suggestion is a shared selection process. Local performers should help define the criteria, and they should be able to review translations before publication. I would also reserve space for collaborations that do not fit a familiar genre label. Cultural identity is not a museum shelf; new work can belong to it as well.\n\nThese conditions would take time and money. We should acknowledge that cost instead of pretending that participation is free. Still, a cheaper agreement that creates lasting resentment might be poor value. I would judge the partnership after one season by asking who participated, who made decisions, and whether performers felt accurately represented. Audience size would matter, but it would not be the only measure of success.',qa:[['What is the curator’s position?','Conditional support','I support the proposed festival partnership, but my support is conditional.',['Conditional support','Complete rejection','Unconditional approval']],['What risk does visibility alone leave unresolved?','A narrow representation without local influence','the community may become visible without gaining much influence',['A narrow representation without local influence','No recordings can travel','All musicians must stop working']],['Which measure would supplement audience size?','Who made decisions','who made decisions',['Who made decisions','Only ticket prices','The shortest performance']]]},
 practiceA:[['Qualify: Globalization destroys local culture.','Globalization may weaken some local practices under particular commercial conditions.'],['Distinguish heritage from a fixed rule against change.','Heritage can inform new work without requiring every practice to remain unchanged.'],['Write a concession about the distributor’s contribution.','Although the distributor offers valuable promotion, performers still need a voice in representation.'],['Identify an unsupported inference: Short formats are requested, so long pieces are banned.','A preference for short formats may disadvantage long pieces, but a ban is not stated.'],['Explain integration without equating it with sameness.','People can participate in shared institutions while retaining different traditions.']],
 practiceB:[['Qualify: International audiences always increase diversity.','International audiences may increase exposure, but selection can still concentrate attention on familiar styles.'],['Distinguish access from authority in one sentence.','Access concerns reaching an audience; authority concerns who decides how the work is presented.'],['Write a concession about shared selection costs.','Shared selection takes resources, yet it may improve legitimacy.'],['Challenge: All elders reject new collaborations.','The case explicitly says that some elders agree with collaboration.'],['Define a fair review criterion beyond audience size.','Performers should be able to judge whether descriptions represent their work accurately.']],
 applyA:[['State a qualified thesis supporting or opposing the proposal.','Accept a clear position with specified conditions rather than an absolute claim.'],['Use one case detail to support your thesis.','Link translation, payment, selection rules or review rights directly to the argument.'],['Present a serious counterargument.','Shared selection can delay work and increase costs.'],['Respond without dismissing that counterargument.','Propose a deadline and a funded local review role.'],['Conclude with a condition that would change your view.','For example, withdrawal of performer review rights would justify rejecting the agreement.']],
 applyB:[['Argue for one local decision that should be protected.','Example: Approval of performance descriptions should involve the performers.'],['Explain one benefit for a musician rather than the town generally.','Payment could support continuing work that local ticket sales do not fund.'],['Critique the assumption that authentic culture cannot change.','The youth council’s collaborations illustrate cultural change from within.'],['Propose a way to evaluate representation.','Invite participating and excluded performers to review selection and descriptions.'],['Write a nuanced final recommendation in two sentences.','Balance opportunity and control; include a feasible condition and acknowledge cost.']],
 activity:'Negotiate the festival agreement',cards:['Performer group: Seek payment and review rights. Some pieces require long storytelling sequences.','Distributor: Offer translation and promotion, but explain the need for clear schedules and manageable costs.','Local committee: Propose selection rules that include established traditions and new collaborations.'],model:'We support the partnership provided performers can review descriptions and local representatives share selection authority. This may slow publication, so we propose one scheduled review round rather than unlimited revisions.',
 challenge:['Explain why a policy that increases visibility might still reduce meaningful diversity.','Write a counterargument that would persuade someone who shares your values.'],caution:'This fictional case represents no real culture. Do not assign one student the role of spokesperson for an entire nationality or community.',homework:['Write a 220–260-word argument about the proposed agreement.','Use two case details, a concession, a counterargument and a conditional recommendation.']
},
{
 slug:'future-of-work',title:'Future of Work',level:'C1',age:'16-18',minutes:75,
 skill:'Speaking',secondary:'Writing',tech:'Projector / screen',style:'Project-based',summary:'Separate tasks from jobs, question assumptions, and defend a realistic training proposal.',
 objective:'Present an evidence-linked proposal for adapting to workplace change, with prioritized skills, a resource trade-off and a measurable review plan.',
 vocab:[['employment','Paid work or the condition of having a job.','Employment involves more than completing isolated tasks.'],['automation','Using a system to carry out tasks with less direct human action.','Automation can change part of a role.'],['adaptability','The ability to respond effectively to changed conditions.','Adaptability includes revising an unsuccessful approach.'],['qualification','A recognized achievement or evidence of competence.','A qualification should demonstrate relevant learning.'],['workforce','The people who work in an organization or economy.','The workforce needs time to learn new processes.']],
 frames:['The evidence supports ___, but does not establish ___ .','We prioritize ___ over ___ because the immediate constraint is ___ .','We would revise this proposal if ___ .'],
 reading:{title:'A Training Plan for Northbridge',text:'Northbridge is a fictional design studio with twelve employees. Its managers are considering a system that drafts routine project summaries from approved notes. Currently, staff spend part of each week producing these summaries and checking them with clients. The proposed system may reduce drafting time, but nobody has yet tested how accurately it handles ambiguous notes or unusual requests. The case does not assume that an entire occupation will disappear.\n\nA pilot team proposes a six-week trial. Staff would compare draft summaries with the original notes, record errors and seek client confirmation before anything is finalized. The trial would therefore change the distribution of tasks: less initial drafting might be balanced by more verification, exception handling and communication. Whether the overall workload falls remains an open question.\n\nThe studio has only two paid training afternoons available during the pilot. One proposal uses both afternoons to teach system operation. A second divides the time between operation and critical review, including how to identify an unsupported conclusion and explain an uncertainty to a client. The second proposal covers fewer features but may address a wider range of risks.\n\nAn employee representative adds that learning outside paid hours would affect workers unequally. Some have caring responsibilities or other commitments. She argues that adaptability should be supported by organizational time and clear procedures, rather than treated solely as an individual personality trait. Managers agree that a certificate alone would not prove competence; employees should demonstrate the skills on realistic examples.\n\nAt the end of six weeks, the team plans to review accuracy, total time spent, client corrections and staff feedback. A faster first draft would not count as success if verification or rework increased substantially. The decision to expand the system will depend on the whole process, not on an impressive demonstration.',qa:[['What has not yet been tested?','Accuracy with ambiguous notes and unusual requests.','nobody has yet tested how accurately it handles ambiguous notes or unusual requests'],['Why might less drafting not reduce total workload?','Verification and other tasks may increase.','less initial drafting might be balanced by more verification, exception handling and communication'],['What is the training constraint?','Two paid afternoons.','The studio has only two paid training afternoons available during the pilot.'],['Why is a certificate insufficient in this case?','Competence should be demonstrated on realistic examples.','employees should demonstrate the skills on realistic examples']]},
 listening:{title:'The Employee Representative’s Proposal',script:'Before we decide which training to buy, I want us to separate two questions. The first is whether staff can operate the new system. The second is whether they can judge its output responsibly. Being able to produce a fluent draft does not establish that the draft is accurate.\n\nI propose using our first training afternoon for basic operation and our second for checking realistic examples. In the review session, people would compare a summary with its source notes, identify unsupported claims and explain unresolved questions to a client. That approach leaves some advanced features for later, which is a real trade-off.\n\nI also want the studio to protect learning time. If employees have to practice after work, those with caring responsibilities may have fewer opportunities. Adaptability should be supported through the way work is organized, not just demanded in a job description.\n\nAfter six weeks, we should review the time spent on the entire process, including corrections. We should also ask whether employees feel able to challenge an unreliable result. If drafting becomes faster but errors take longer to resolve, we should revise the plan. The goal is a more reliable service and a workforce that can exercise informed judgment, rather than a certificate that everyone receives for attending.',qa:[['How would the two afternoons be used?','Operation, then checking realistic examples','our first training afternoon for basic operation and our second for checking realistic examples',['Operation, then checking realistic examples','Two afternoons of advanced features','Only certificate preparation']],['What trade-off does the proposal acknowledge?','Some advanced features are postponed','That approach leaves some advanced features for later',['Some advanced features are postponed','All clients leave','Staff stop learning']],['What should trigger revision?','Errors take longer to resolve despite faster drafting','If drafting becomes faster but errors take longer to resolve, we should revise the plan.',['Errors take longer to resolve despite faster drafting','Everyone attends training','Staff ask useful questions']]]},
 practiceA:[['Correct the overclaim: Automated summaries will eliminate design jobs.','The pilot concerns summary drafting; the case does not establish job elimination.'],['Distinguish a qualification from demonstrated competence.','A qualification records an achievement; competence requires evidence of effective performance.'],['Name a task likely to remain important in the proposed pilot.','Checking a draft against source notes or clarifying uncertainty with a client.'],['Write a measurable question about total workload.','How does total drafting, checking and correction time compare with the previous process?'],['Explain one equity concern about unpaid training.','Employees with caring responsibilities may have less time to participate.']],
 practiceB:[['Qualify: Fast output means successful automation.','Fast output is useful only if accuracy and downstream work remain acceptable.'],['Turn adaptability into a specific observable skill.','Revise a checking procedure after identifying a recurring error.'],['Name a trade-off in the two-afternoon plan.','Time spent on critical review reduces time for advanced system features.'],['Specify an assessment beyond attendance.','Compare a draft with notes and explain two unsupported claims correctly.'],['Write a condition for delaying expansion.','Delay expansion if total correction time rises or serious errors persist.']],
 applyA:[['Prioritize two training skills and explain the choice.','Basic operation and critical review address immediate use and reliability risks.'],['Allocate the two paid afternoons.','One operation workshop and one applied review workshop, with clear tasks.'],['Choose two process measures.','Total completion time and confirmed error rate; define how each is recorded.'],['Include a worker participation safeguard.','Collect confidential feedback during paid time and allow staff to flag unreliable output.'],['Present a conditional recommendation.','Proceed with a limited pilot, subject to accuracy, workload and participation checks.']],
 applyB:[['Design one realistic competence check.','Provide source notes and a flawed summary; assess detection, correction and explanation.'],['Explain why a job is more than one task.','The role also includes judgment, communication and handling unusual cases.'],['Identify one uncertainty the pilot can address.','Whether the system handles ambiguous notes accurately.'],['Identify one claim this small pilot cannot establish.','A general prediction about employment across the whole economy.'],['Defend a revision rule in a one-minute pitch.','State a threshold or review criterion, explain its purpose and avoid claiming unsupported forecasts.']],
 activity:'Pitch a six-week training pilot',cards:['Studio constraint: Twelve employees; two paid training afternoons; a six-week pilot.','Deliverable: A one-page proposal with two skills, a schedule, an assessment and a review rule.','Review panel: Question one assumption, one resource trade-off and the difference between task speed and overall service quality.'],model:'We propose operation training followed by critical review. Employees will demonstrate checking on realistic notes. We will compare total processing time and confirmed errors after six weeks, and postpone expansion if reliability deteriorates.',
 challenge:['Redesign the proposal if only one training afternoon is approved.','Explain what evidence would justify reversing your recommendation.'],caution:'Present the studio as a fictional scenario, not a forecast about any real job market. Students may use imagined career interests.',homework:['Write a 250–300-word proposal using the group’s pilot plan.','Include a limitation, a cost in staff time, and a clear review rule.']
},
{
 slug:'inequality-and-social-mobility',title:'Inequality and Social Mobility',level:'C2',age:'Adults (20-23)',minutes:90,
 skill:'Writing',secondary:'Critical Thinking',tech:'Full technology (internet, devices, audio)',style:'Debate-based',summary:'Evaluate causal claims, distinguish outcomes from mechanisms, and defend a conditional policy judgment.',
 objective:'Write a nuanced policy analysis that separates causal hypotheses from evidence, weighs distributional trade-offs and specifies what would justify revision.',
 vocab:[['inequality','An uneven distribution of resources, conditions or outcomes.','Inequality can persist even when a formal rule is equal.'],['mobility','Movement between social or economic positions.','A mobility measure needs a defined comparison and period.'],['systemic','Arising from the organization of connected institutions or practices.','A systemic barrier may persist without an explicit exclusion rule.'],['opportunity','A meaningful chance to pursue an outcome.','Opportunity depends partly on whether access is usable.'],['policy','An organized course of action adopted by an institution.','A policy can have different effects on different groups.']],
 frames:['The observed association is consistent with ___, but cannot by itself establish ___ .','The policy’s merits depend partly on how ___ is distributed and measured.','Even if ___, it would not follow that ___; an alternative explanation is ___ .'],
 reading:{title:'Equal Eligibility, Unequal Access',text:'The following municipality, program and observations are fictional. Arden offers a training grant to residents seeking a new qualification. Anyone meeting the same published income and residency criteria may apply. Officials initially describe the policy as equal access because the eligibility rule does not distinguish between neighborhoods. Yet application and completion patterns differ across the municipality. The available case notes report the difference without supplying a representative dataset or identifying its cause.\n\nOne explanation emphasizes individual choices: eligible residents may value the course differently or prefer immediate work. Another points to institutional conditions. Classes end after the last bus to two neighborhoods, some applicants lack reliable childcare, and the application requires documents from several offices. These mechanisms could constrain participation even where motivation is strong. Their plausibility, however, is not proof that they explain every non-application or withdrawal.\n\nA proposed universal transport allowance would be simple to administer and avoid requiring participants to demonstrate exceptional need. Critics argue that some funds would go to people who already have adequate transport. A targeted package of evening transport and childcare support might direct more resources toward specific barriers, but it could introduce intrusive eligibility checks, administrative delays and errors that exclude people it is meant to help. A third proposal simplifies the application process for everyone, although simplification alone would not change the bus schedule.\n\nThe council must also decide what success means. More applications would show increased take-up, not necessarily qualification completion or lasting mobility. Higher completion among participants would be encouraging, but selection into the program could make a comparison with non-participants misleading. A credible evaluation would need a defensible comparison, attention to who remains excluded and a follow-up period suited to the outcome being claimed.\n\nA balanced recommendation can therefore support action while remaining explicit about uncertainty. The absence of decisive causal evidence need not justify inaction, but neither does a plausible systemic explanation license certainty about a particular remedy. The policy should connect each intervention to an identified barrier, make its distributional choices visible and establish conditions for revision. Equal eligibility is a starting rule; whether it yields meaningful opportunity is an empirical and normative question.',qa:[['Why does the case not establish the cause of different participation rates?','It provides neither a representative dataset nor a causal identification.','without supplying a representative dataset or identifying its cause'],['Give one mechanism that could restrict participation despite motivation.','Classes finish after the last bus to two neighborhoods.','Classes end after the last bus to two neighborhoods'],['What is the main administrative trade-off of targeting?','It may focus resources but add intrusive checks, delays and exclusion errors.','it could introduce intrusive eligibility checks, administrative delays and errors'],['Why are more applications insufficient evidence of lasting mobility?','Take-up is distinct from completion and longer-term outcomes.','More applications would show increased take-up, not necessarily qualification completion or lasting mobility.']]},
 listening:{title:'The Evaluation Adviser’s Caution',script:'I would resist two tempting conclusions about Arden’s training grant. One is that equal eligibility proves equal opportunity. The other is that unequal participation proves a single systemic cause. The case supports neither conclusion on its own. It gives us plausible mechanisms that deserve investigation, not a complete causal account.\n\nMy recommendation is to simplify the application for everyone while piloting evening transport where the timetable creates an identified barrier. Childcare support should also be considered, but its design needs consultation with prospective participants. We should record who cannot use each intervention and why.\n\nEvaluation must distinguish take-up, completion and longer-term outcomes. An increase in applications would not, by itself, demonstrate social mobility. Nor would a higher completion rate among participants prove that the grant caused the difference, since people who join may differ from those who do not.\n\nThere is also a value judgment that evidence cannot make for us: how much administrative simplicity we are willing to exchange for more targeted support. That judgment should be stated openly. I would support a limited, reviewable policy with a clear comparison strategy, rather than promise a permanent solution before we understand the mechanisms. Uncertainty is a reason to design learning into the policy, not a reason to disguise assumptions as findings.',qa:[['Which two claims does the adviser reject as established conclusions?','Equal eligibility proves opportunity; unequal participation proves one systemic cause','The case supports neither conclusion on its own.',['Equal eligibility proves opportunity; unequal participation proves one systemic cause','All grants work; all transport fails','Qualifications are useless; all residents are identical']],['Why is a simple participant comparison insufficient?','People who join may differ from those who do not','people who join may differ from those who do not',['People who join may differ from those who do not','Completion cannot be measured','No one applied']],['What trade-off requires an explicit value judgment?','Administrative simplicity versus more targeted support','how much administrative simplicity we are willing to exchange for more targeted support',['Administrative simplicity versus more targeted support','Whether buses exist','The name of the municipality']]]},
 practiceA:[['Distinguish an observed disparity from an established cause.','A disparity describes a difference; a causal claim needs evidence about the mechanism and alternatives.'],['Qualify: Non-participation shows a lack of ambition.','Non-participation may reflect preferences or constraints; the case does not isolate their contributions.'],['Identify a systemic mechanism without attributing malicious intent.','The class schedule and bus timetable may jointly prevent attendance.'],['Separate an empirical claim from a normative judgment about targeting.','Whether targeting reaches excluded residents is empirical; how much intrusion is acceptable is normative.'],['Explain why take-up is not equivalent to mobility.','Applying does not establish completion or a sustained change in social or economic position.']],
 practiceB:[['Correct: Equal eligibility guarantees equal opportunity.','Identical formal eligibility may coexist with unequal practical access.'],['State a competing explanation for higher participant completion.','Participants may begin with different resources or readiness.'],['Explain an exclusion error in a targeted policy.','Someone who needs support may be incorrectly judged ineligible or unable to complete the process.'],['Give a reason universal provision might be preferred despite leakage.','It may reduce administrative burden and avoid intrusive checks.'],['Describe a defensible revision rule without invented evidence.','Review the pilot if intended participants remain unable to attend, while investigating why.']],
 applyA:[['Formulate a qualified causal hypothesis about the bus schedule.','Late class times may reduce attendance for residents dependent on the last bus.'],['Identify evidence that would weaken that hypothesis.','Affected residents may report and demonstrate a different dominant barrier; check credible attendance and transport information.'],['Compare universal and targeted support on two dimensions.','Compare reach and administrative burden, while noting exclusion and unused support.'],['Specify an outcome and suitable follow-up conceptually.','Define completion first and a later work or income outcome; do not equate immediate take-up with mobility.'],['Write a conditional policy thesis that admits uncertainty.','Support a limited intervention tied to identified barriers, with evaluation and revision conditions.']],
 applyB:[['Steelman the case for individual choice as one explanation.','Residents may reasonably prefer immediate work or different training, even when access is practical.'],['Explain why that argument does not eliminate systemic constraints.','Preferences and institutional barriers can operate simultaneously.'],['Design one nonintrusive question for prospective participants.','Which scheduling or travel arrangements would make attendance possible for you?'],['State a limit of the fictional evidence.','No representative dataset or identified causal effect is supplied.'],['Defend an evaluation strategy while acknowledging an ethical constraint.','Seek a credible comparison without deliberately removing existing support; explain remaining selection limitations.']],
 activity:'A policy hearing with a written dissent',cards:['Council group: Recommend a limited package using the fictional case only. State whose access improves and who may remain excluded.','Review group: Test the causal assumptions and administrative trade-offs. Do not treat a plausible mechanism as a measured effect.','Final task: Write a joint recommendation and a short dissent that accurately represents the strongest unresolved objection.'],model:'We support simplified applications and a reviewable evening-transport pilot because both address specified barriers. This is a provisional judgment, not a demonstrated mobility effect. Evaluation should distinguish take-up, completion and later outcomes while documenting who remains excluded.',
 challenge:['Explain how the same outcome pattern could arise from two different mechanisms.','Write a dissent that accepts your opponent’s factual premises but disputes the policy trade-off.'],caution:'Use the fictional case, not classmates’ family income or personal experiences. No external research is required; optional sources must be distinguished from the supplied evidence.',homework:['Write a 320–380-word policy analysis with a 60-word dissent.','Include a rival explanation, a distributional trade-off, an evaluation limit and a revision condition.']
}
